{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01RKTG8oHCi8xGgv59tZFAt9","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t//! Generic slotmap-style arena.\n2\t//!\n3\t//! A storage container that hands out [`Token`]s as opaque handles\n4\t//! to its values.  Tokens carry a generation counter so that\n5\t//! reusing a slot after removal invalidates any outstanding tokens\n6\t//! — the safe equivalent of the C `*mut T` use-after-free.\n7\t//!\n8\t//! Every Phase-4 collection that needs \"address an object by\n9\t//! handle, not by raw pointer\" sits on top of this — most notably\n10\t//! the `Quic` connection arena, which holds every live connection\n11\t//! and lets the secondary indexes (`connection_by_id`, `connection_by_net`, …)\n12\t//! store `ConnectionToken`s as their values.\n13\t\n14\tuse core::marker::PhantomData;\n15\t\n16\tuse crate::Error;\n17\t\n18\t/// Opaque handle into an [`Arena<T>`].  `Copy`, 64 bits total.\n19\t///\n20\t/// The `T` parameter is purely a phantom — it stops you from using\n21\t/// a `Token<Connection>` to index into an `Arena<Stream>`.  The\n22\t/// runtime check still lives in the generation counter inside the\n23\t/// arena.\n24\t#[derive(Debug)]\n25\tpub struct Token<T> {\n26\t    idx: u32,\n27\t    generation: u32,\n28\t    _phantom: PhantomData<fn() -> T>,\n29\t}\n30\t\n31\t// Manual PartialEq/Eq/Hash so that `Token<T>` works even when `T` is not\n32\t// `PartialEq`.  Two tokens are equal when their physical slot index and\n33\t// generation counter match — the `T` phantom plays no role at runtime.\n34\timpl<T> PartialEq for Token<T> {\n35\t    fn eq(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {\n36\t        self.idx == other.idx && self.generation == other.generation\n37\t    }\n38\t}\n39\t\n40\timpl<T> Eq for Token<T> {}\n41\t\n42\timpl<T> core::hash::Hash for Token<T> {\n43\t    fn hash<H: core::hash::Hasher>(&self, state: &mut H) {\n44\t        self.idx.hash(state);\n45\t        self.generation.hash(state);\n46\t    }\n47\t}\n48\t\n49\t// `Copy` and `Clone` written by hand so they don't depend on `T:\n50\t// Copy`.  Tokens are always `Copy` regardless of what `T` is."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e85c7bed-bd05-4049-931b-16153c644e2c","uuid":"d8a7cc8f-ff02-438f-81d1-e0e2e505606c","timestamp":"2026-05-07T09:11:51.963Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/Users/richbarn/Projects/picoquic/rs/fq/src/arena.rs","content":"//! Generic slotmap-style arena.\n//!\n//! A storage container that hands out [`Token`]s as opaque handles\n//! to its values.  Tokens carry a generation counter so that\n//! reusing a slot after removal invalidates any outstanding tokens\n//! — the safe equivalent of the C `*mut T` use-after-free.\n//!\n//! Every Phase-4 collection that needs \"address an object by\n//! handle, not by raw pointer\" sits on top of this — most notably\n//! the `Quic` connection arena, which holds every live connection\n//! and lets the secondary indexes (`connection_by_id`, `connection_by_net`, …)\n//! store `ConnectionToken`s as their values.\n\nuse core::marker::PhantomData;\n\nuse crate::Error;\n\n/// Opaque handle into an [`Arena<T>`].  `Copy`, 64 bits total.\n///\n/// The `T` parameter is purely a phantom — it stops you from using\n/// a `Token<Connection>` to index into an `Arena<Stream>`.  The\n/// runtime check still lives in the generation counter inside the\n/// arena.\n#[derive(Debug)]\npub struct Token<T> {\n    idx: u32,\n    generation: u32,\n    _phantom: PhantomData<fn() -> T>,\n}\n\n// Manual PartialEq/Eq/Hash so that `Token<T>` works even when `T` is not\n// `PartialEq`.  Two tokens are equal when their physical slot index and\n// generation counter match — the `T` phantom plays no role at runtime.\nimpl<T> PartialEq for Token<T> {\n    fn eq(&self, other: &Self) -> bool {\n        self.idx == other.idx && self.generation == other.generation\n    }\n}\n\nimpl<T> Eq for Token<T> {}\n\nimpl<T> core::hash::Hash for Token<T> {\n    fn hash<H: core::hash::Hasher>(&self, state: &mut H) {\n        self.idx.hash(state);\n        self.generation.hash(state);\n    }\n}\n\n// `Copy` and `Clone` written by hand so they don't depend on `T:\n// Copy`.  Tokens are always `Copy` regardless of what `T` is.","numLines":50,"startLine":1,"totalLines":290}}}
